Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system, with Bruhat order $\le$ on $W$ and identity element $e$. For each pair $u,v \in W$ with $u \le v$, let $P_{u,v}(q) \in \mathbb{Z}[q]$ denote the associated Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial, defined as the unique family of polynomials satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $P_{u,u}(q) = 1$ for all $u \in W$, and $P_{u,v}(q) = 0$ unless $u \le v$;
\item $\deg P_{u,v}(q) \le \tfrac{1}{2}(\ell(v) - \ell(u) - 1)$ when $u < v$, where $\ell$ is the Coxeter length;
\item the recursion arising from the Hecke algebra of $(W,S)$, equivalently
$$q^{\ell(v)-\ell(u)} P_{u,v}(q^{-1}) = \sum_{u \le z \le v} R_{u,z}(q)\, P_{z,v}(q),$$
where $R_{u,z}(q)$ are the Kazhdan–Lusztig $R$-polynomials.
\end{enumerate}

Question: For every Coxeter system $(W,S)$ and every pair $u,v \in W$ with $u \le v$, do there exist a Coxeter system $(W',S')$ and an element $w \in W'$ such that
$$P_{u,v}(q) \;=\; P_{e,w}(q)\quad \text{in } \mathbb{Z}[q],$$
where $e$ denotes the identity of $W'$?

